#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198047 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bbc1e6a-c728-5c5a-84f8-06a92c30a023" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With No Inner Critical Points"}] :question/id #uuid "fb947756-3471-59c8-b3c4-9b21a1a91557" :question/math-academy-id "e-12457" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + y$ given that $2x^2 + 3y^2 \\leq 1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= 2x^2 + 3y^2  = 1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,6y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=4\\lambda x \\\\ 1 &=6\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ \\lambda y &=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  = 1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)^{2}+3{y}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3} \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda = \\dfrac12$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}y = \\dfrac{1}{6} \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad y = \\dfrac1{3}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  =1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+3{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3} \\right), \\qquad  \\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $\\left(0, \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$ | $\\left(0, -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$ | $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3}$ | $-\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right),$ and its maximum value is $\\dfrac23.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198240 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198366 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffdd4f56-003d-5eed-a60d-ba51c4c86b51" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With Inner Critical Points"}] :question/id #uuid "7d0da02a-d686-5bb1-ab44-00286ab01783" :question/math-academy-id "e-8690" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y) = 3x^2 - y^2$ given that $3x^2 + 2y^2\\leq 16.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨6x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=\\left(0,  0 \\right).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= 3x^2 + 2y^2  = 16.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=16\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨6x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨6x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x &=6\\lambda x \\\\ -2y &=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(2\\lambda +1) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  =16$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2} & =16\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  =16$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & =16\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{4\\sqrt 3}{3}, 0 \\right).$\n\n- Note that assuming $\\lambda = 1$ from the first equation leads to $y=0,$ which we already have a solution for, and $\\lambda = -\\dfrac12$ from the second equation leads to $x=0,$ which we also have a solution for. So, we have found all the candidates on the boundary.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left( 0,0 \\right), \\qquad  \\left( 0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right), \\qquad \\left(\\pm \\dfrac{4\\sqrt 3}{3}, 0\\right).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $\\left( 0,0 \\right)$ | $\\left( 0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right)$ | $\\left( \\pm \\dfrac{4\\sqrt 3}{3}, 0 \\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-8$ | $16$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $\\left(0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2  \\right),$ and its minimum value is $-8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198241 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198367 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "849449fb-bb08-54a3-b8ea-9f33ddd7a3e5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Optimization Problems in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "9235c493-f092-5f14-b989-be3b4548a90d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8691" :question/problem "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, find the maximum value of the sum of cosines of the angle measures of a triangle, given that all three angles are acute." :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = \\cos{x}+\\cos{y}+\\cos{z},\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\pi.\n$$\n\nSince the angles are acute, we also have the constraints $0 \\lt x,y,z \\lt \\dfrac\\pi 2.$ \n\nLet $g(x,y,z) = x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y,z), \\qquad g(x,y,z) = \\pi\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x &=\\lambda \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡y &=\\lambda \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡z &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sine function is one-to-one on the interval $\\left( 0, \\dfrac \\pi 2\\right),$ we can conclude that $x = y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y =x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =\\pi  \\\\ x+x+x & =\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's easy to show that this point is indeed the maximum and not the minimum. To do that, we can compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & ≈1.47 \\\\  & <f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bbc1e6a-c728-5c5a-84f8-06a92c30a023" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With No Inner Critical Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "516e7492-e083-54f4-b969-813cd1e7e379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446758} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b0972e-d4be-5359-ad60-bb6bb0f92728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446784} {:answer/id #uuid "c11354ec-35c7-5aa0-87c4-54a1c16aac24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446787} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a79e2b-437a-5e9b-ac4c-f92bd6d086fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446788} {:answer/id #uuid "d21bbc09-d6e4-59ee-99da-258932620c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d21bbc09-d6e4-59ee-99da-258932620c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446791} :answer-field/id #uuid "414dce70-34d5-521d-80c0-bd1e958ec286" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74a32659-ab2b-5195-9c2f-351117b323e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162778" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y$ given that ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2 &=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ \\lambda y &=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system gives \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{2}).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})$ | $(0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})$ | $(\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{2}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198367 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "849449fb-bb08-54a3-b8ea-9f33ddd7a3e5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Optimization Problems in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f121e3-ccfa-57b8-b6ef-a2e2cd0f3b57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278810} {:answer/id #uuid "65b8a4fe-1d29-5ee2-ad9f-6c0583668e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279516} {:answer/id #uuid "76c3a733-f198-5a7c-a7fb-0c39f1adc2a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279650} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf6ea47-eaeb-5270-89ec-aea8d1830cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279837} {:answer/id #uuid "944923f9-e97d-5493-8850-5c83f2a0ed3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65b8a4fe-1d29-5ee2-ad9f-6c0583668e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279516} :answer-field/id #uuid "797a794d-3c44-5cfd-9959-01ae83cb5934" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8c68071-089c-5bff-bc03-ba61a8eab03b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162788" :question/problem "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, find the minimum possible sum of three positive numbers whose product is $27.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the three numbers. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+y+z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=27.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=27\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=\\lambda yz \\\\ 1 &=\\lambda xz \\\\ 1 &=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this would give $1=0.$ Therefore, from the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}yz & =xz \\\\ yz-xz & =0 \\\\ (y-x)z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z>0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y=x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =27 \\\\ x(x)(x) & =27 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =27 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(3,3,3),$ and the minimum value is \n\n$$\nf(3,3,3)=3+3+3=9.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(9,3,1) & =9+3+1 \\\\  & =13 \\\\  & >f(3,3,3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198366 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffdd4f56-003d-5eed-a60d-ba51c4c86b51" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With Inner Critical Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4733a3dd-e23c-5c44-a89f-742c3f778029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389362} {:answer/id #uuid "88e50d0f-e873-5eb6-8df9-c6576dfee96e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389363} {:answer/id #uuid "af614f6a-7d1a-5c02-b853-cbc404a8abf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389364} {:answer/id #uuid "ab93d64e-a39e-5c1d-a6e2-ae62934b68a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{23}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389365} {:answer/id #uuid "d5db156c-c947-5d50-8157-91edc1a6189c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5db156c-c947-5d50-8157-91edc1a6189c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389366} :answer-field/id #uuid "99385998-af51-5830-b0cc-4d03c327bc9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0ad6e6a-85b2-5b87-87b2-edb5a819eb6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162789" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y$ given that ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\le 9.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y-2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,1).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=9.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=9\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y-2 &=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda -1) &=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1) &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=9$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, this doesn't give any critical point.\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation gives $y=\\frac{1}{2},$ and the constraint condition $g(x,y)=9$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4} & =9\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{37}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{37}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,1),\\,(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{37}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,1)$ | $(\\frac{\\pm \\sqrt{37}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{17}{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,1),$ and its minimum value is $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198367 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "849449fb-bb08-54a3-b8ea-9f33ddd7a3e5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Optimization Problems in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c1a0328-21a2-5d4a-be72-a0e945edf28f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446751} {:answer/id #uuid "4026557b-c261-56b2-bc95-94093b494f38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446754} {:answer/id #uuid "502949fc-7dbe-5b07-85d8-6afa9e12632d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446757} {:answer/id #uuid "79fcac3f-eebf-518e-b439-901998e02ca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446767} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd42a19-38cc-5465-a5d7-71dd32273277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4026557b-c261-56b2-bc95-94093b494f38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446754} :answer-field/id #uuid "d16c3309-85b6-5b20-bebc-f505a6812eb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "71aa525f-c293-54c2-9046-61ecb78eb1e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-162796" :question/problem "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, find the maximum value of the sum of the cosines of three non-negative angles whose sum is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nWe also have the constraints $0\\le x,y,z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x &=\\lambda \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡y &=\\lambda \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡z &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sine function is one-to-one on the interval $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y=x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ x+x+x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 3x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0,0) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & <f(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198366 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffdd4f56-003d-5eed-a60d-ba51c4c86b51" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With Inner Critical Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92fa23c5-f5ca-52b3-b9c6-f9d5a4e10d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363467} {:answer/id #uuid "96364a64-4346-5e93-8663-7f3c260832d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363468} {:answer/id #uuid "0cbfda04-b7ed-5845-b354-cec468c5c6bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363469} {:answer/id #uuid "b806c0e9-8a1c-528e-a67e-89e1eba6df52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363470} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc8ece8-ce75-5ec7-a65b-0906f086e127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cbfda04-b7ed-5845-b354-cec468c5c6bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363469} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ca7b7a8-4b09-595e-a90a-557478166188" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0965ed17-df89-5de8-b0cd-60d291248cdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-162797" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+x+y$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x+1,2y+1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global maximum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=3\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x+1,2y+1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =1$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 1,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y+1 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{2(\\lambda -1)} \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2(\\lambda -1)}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ in the condition $g(x,y)=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}),\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2})$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $3-\\sqrt{6}$ | $3+\\sqrt{6}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}),$ and its maximum value is $3+\\sqrt{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bbc1e6a-c728-5c5a-84f8-06a92c30a023" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With No Inner Critical Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46eba19c-bd0e-522f-bc7b-c12557272eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279223} {:answer/id #uuid "a60479c7-89b2-57c2-88e4-9d540655daaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280063} {:answer/id #uuid "dbed659a-82b2-5099-8848-e6d48d786d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280535} {:answer/id #uuid "df85d671-e54e-5517-929b-ccad67dc2cb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280564} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5a0365-1bf5-5b8f-87f4-9cc3c08f1119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46eba19c-bd0e-522f-bc7b-c12557272eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279223} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c669944-f272-5496-a15d-0f74257cf2f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "172e1cdb-aee2-5490-b82d-56a90e36d5c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162801" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ given that $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨8x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $x=y=0$ which yields the critical point $(0,0).$ Notice that this point is not in the region's interior. However, it is in the boundary of the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2(x-1),2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8x &=2\\lambda (x-1) \\\\ -2y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x &=\\lambda (x-1) \\\\ y(\\lambda +1) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=1\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(2,0)$ and $(0,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies \n\n$$\n4x=-(x-1)\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{5}-1)}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\frac{1}{5},\\pm \\frac{3}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(\\frac{1}{5},\\pm \\frac{3}{5}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(\\frac{1}{5},\\pm \\frac{3}{5})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $16$ | $-\\frac{1}{5}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(2,0),$ and its maximum value is $16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bbc1e6a-c728-5c5a-84f8-06a92c30a023" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With No Inner Critical Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "114e2c5c-e9dd-526c-ae0c-f4bd4455b974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278752} {:answer/id #uuid "43b3f900-4df0-5da7-a38e-25a5c0664f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279199} {:answer/id #uuid "45f90586-57c8-5cb9-a773-850a108afbb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279216} {:answer/id #uuid "513449f1-9cde-56ba-bba6-c67edd180332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279332} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2cc5a0-0ccb-5606-a4dc-e9f5799eb33a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca2cc5a0-0ccb-5606-a4dc-e9f5799eb33a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280363} :answer-field/id #uuid "7440ee8d-881a-5b79-93db-d064eac73e7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "46d9a0e8-afba-50ff-93a4-8a5c09128d7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162802" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ given that $4{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $x=y=0$ which yields the critical point $(0,0).$ Notice that this point is not in the region's interior. However, it is in the boundary of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1.$\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8x,2(y-1)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=8\\lambda x \\\\ -2y &=2\\lambda (y-1)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-4\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y &=-\\lambda (y-1)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=1\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0)$ and $(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{4}(y-1)\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{5}-1)}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{3}{10},\\frac{1}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\pm \\frac{3}{10},\\frac{1}{5})\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(\\pm \\frac{3}{10},\\frac{1}{5})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{20}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,2),$ and its minimum value is $-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198366 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffdd4f56-003d-5eed-a60d-ba51c4c86b51" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With Inner Critical Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d0a81d8-13d7-582a-8b41-a392595376a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278989} {:answer/id #uuid "4317249d-bf8c-51ae-832f-3ccae3822035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279194} {:answer/id #uuid "48a08e81-e9f4-555b-bc1a-45947a7f7606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279240} {:answer/id #uuid "5c59dc60-4505-5c4d-9a47-aa9bbfb5b511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279440} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3398bc-3678-517d-9759-8a6544ab7136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4317249d-bf8c-51ae-832f-3ccae3822035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279194} :answer-field/id #uuid "586bad13-066b-5c7d-b740-b16bd4206a08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "78ee316a-eb04-5e68-af98-0ccef9f21d29" :question/math-academy-id "q-163055" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-6$ given that $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}\\le 5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}=5.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨\\frac{x}{2},-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x &=\\frac{\\lambda x}{2} \\\\ 2y &=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda +2) &=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- If $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, then assuming the constraint condition $g(x,y)=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =5\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\sqrt{20},0).$\n\n- If $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, then from the first equation we have $x=0$, and assuming the constraint condition $g(x,y)=5$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2} & =5\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not possible.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(\\pm \\sqrt{20},0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(\\pm \\sqrt{20},0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-6$ | $-16$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,0),$ and its maximum value is $-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bbc1e6a-c728-5c5a-84f8-06a92c30a023" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With No Inner Critical Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17db0ffb-b770-5981-ad25-771eba941c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197968} {:answer/id #uuid "3b386300-4c24-597f-9204-ff433d9b9e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197969} {:answer/id #uuid "7b766f86-afe6-5a6b-af92-1c826d2db774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446769} {:answer/id #uuid "9cff40e6-e6af-52b0-9794-92138bab247c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446776} {:answer/id #uuid "ada4dc81-6317-551f-a35f-ec559d59d817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ada4dc81-6317-551f-a35f-ec559d59d817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446779} :answer-field/id #uuid "943b68fc-e739-5e05-8b0d-6d7886409b6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76ff6072-ae0f-589d-9844-098ccc148f05" :question/math-academy-id "q-98102" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=10+x+y$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical points in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\ny=x=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $10+\\sqrt{2}$ | $10-\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),$ and its minimum value is $10-\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199018 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198367 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "849449fb-bb08-54a3-b8ea-9f33ddd7a3e5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Optimization Problems in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "294a6497-3763-569f-bdf9-42356cd00564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54{\\text{ cm}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361019} {:answer/id #uuid "52b8263c-0e8f-52be-ba17-ae87e84b31f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32{\\text{ cm}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361202} {:answer/id #uuid "52e615b0-5300-5122-b210-2d048d12e825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48{\\text{ cm}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361204} {:answer/id #uuid "96f252cb-20d0-588c-88d0-dd5df5d6f960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24{\\text{ cm}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361510} {:answer/id #uuid "ed3d1ba2-d9d5-5815-a75e-960a8bfa5fbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36{\\text{ cm}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52b8263c-0e8f-52be-ba17-ae87e84b31f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32{\\text{ cm}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361202} :answer-field/id #uuid "62a25a4a-3331-5eca-802d-4fffe8f3048b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c427251e-04fb-5644-a763-9c09169a9227" :question/math-academy-id "q-98104" :question/problem "A rectangular box without a lid has a surface area of $48{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$ Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum possible volume of the box." :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y$, and $z$ be the box's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xyz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\n2xz+2yz+xy=48.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=2xz+2yz+xy.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=48\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2z+y,2z+x,2x+2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}yz &=\\lambda (2z+y) \\\\ xz &=\\lambda (2z+x) \\\\ xy &=\\lambda (2x+2y)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}xyz &=\\lambda x(2z+y) \\\\ xyz &=\\lambda y(2z+x) \\\\ xyz &=\\lambda z(2x+2y)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this would give $xyz=0,$ which contradicts the constraints. Therefore, from the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2z+y) & =y(2z+x) \\\\ 2xz+xy & =2yz+xy \\\\ xz & =yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z\\ne 0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $x=y=2z.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xz+2yz+xy & =48 \\\\ 4{z}^{2}+4{z}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =48 \\\\ 12{z}^{2} & =48 \\\\ z & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(4,4,2),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\nf(4,4,2)=4\\cdot 4\\cdot 2=32\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2,5.5) & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot 5.5 \\\\  & =22 \\\\  & <f(4,4,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198367 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "849449fb-bb08-54a3-b8ea-9f33ddd7a3e5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Optimization Problems in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ccbc00-9325-556b-a2d6-4c11af9d456e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3+\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446747} {:answer/id #uuid "5ea94620-3d10-5b50-b37f-bb7f9b5dfd37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446760} {:answer/id #uuid "633619bf-d174-511b-822e-3e895a240fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446761} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf3e77a-01de-506d-9a3b-29ed6a80b5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446789} {:answer/id #uuid "fe28be55-c646-5b66-9d92-47cfae8c06d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe28be55-c646-5b66-9d92-47cfae8c06d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446798} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cb2db16-47e5-539e-a0cd-d00a57818d78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "302ed073-7778-5ce1-8530-7759debd7de4" :question/math-academy-id "q-99203" :question/problem "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the maximum value of the sum of the sines of angle measures of a triangle." :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles of the triangle. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $0<x,y,z<\\pi .$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=\\pi \n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\operatorname{cos}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x &=\\lambda \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y &=\\lambda \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡z &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the cosine function is one-to-one on the interval $(0,\\pi ),$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y=x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =\\pi  \\\\ x+x+x & =\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1+\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & <f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198367 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "849449fb-bb08-54a3-b8ea-9f33ddd7a3e5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Optimization Problems in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2994103a-4373-5061-96dc-5e3036bdaad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446749} {:answer/id #uuid "8f6c4308-b2fa-5fef-b8c1-c5199b6fd2aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446774} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b6bde5-927c-502e-9679-dbf365afa213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446777} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f2b83f-9511-5453-b261-063e160979bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446783} {:answer/id #uuid "bab5edc3-664a-5cc1-98e9-141f93a0f45d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2994103a-4373-5061-96dc-5e3036bdaad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446749} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dba08d8-907e-5202-bcd7-a010c639be80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1eb20554-7ec2-5620-86b4-093c81339236" :question/math-academy-id "q-99235" :question/problem "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the maximum sum of the square roots of three positive numbers given that the sum of their squares is $27.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be three positive numbers. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}+\\sqrt{z},\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=27.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=27\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}},\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} &=2\\lambda x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} &=2\\lambda y \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}} &=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nEquivalently, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-3/2} &=4\\lambda \\\\ {y}^{-3/2} &=4\\lambda \\\\ {z}^{-3/2} &=4\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$\n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =27 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =27 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =27 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(3,3,3),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\nf(3,3,3)=\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}=3\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5,1,1) & =\\sqrt{5}+1+1 \\\\  & =2+\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & <f(3,3,3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362348 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198366 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffdd4f56-003d-5eed-a60d-ba51c4c86b51" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With Inner Critical Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e208ac1-85a1-5e96-b7fa-52323bca0603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361546} {:answer/id #uuid "a36edd82-90fe-5174-9433-626f33ab66c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361567} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe3af3e-8a8f-5c90-9dc6-fba808b09e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361694} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a5aea1-7458-5a36-bfcd-ed941a842039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361700} {:answer/id #uuid "ca55e0f9-7162-5f22-b015-8e0165d10253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfe3af3e-8a8f-5c90-9dc6-fba808b09e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361694} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a1f27a1-0eda-517d-a4eb-016e3810f8d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37949110-2c01-56b7-86c7-c788694289e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-99255" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+10{y}^{2}$ on the region ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ \n\n*Hint*: The critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4$ is $(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNow, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8x,20y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 20y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(4-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(10-\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 2).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2),\\,(\\pm 2,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 2)$ | $(\\pm 2,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $40$ | $16$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,2)$ and $(0,-2),$ and its maximum value is $40.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198366 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffdd4f56-003d-5eed-a60d-ba51c4c86b51" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With Inner Critical Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "940363d2-e432-556a-b0c0-2c42895c1117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198003} {:answer/id #uuid "20e54280-ff88-5af2-8610-6ef05e375392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198004} {:answer/id #uuid "2740310c-d926-5b3d-916c-6755f45ae1b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198005} {:answer/id #uuid "5bdb73f9-d522-5716-a40d-01eb373017ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198006} {:answer/id #uuid "70c9a7e7-b1ad-5149-9703-aa84c63a4445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bdb73f9-d522-5716-a40d-01eb373017ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198006} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd5a22f3-e62c-5edf-b899-6619cb4a4071" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73adc7f1-2e5c-592b-b8c9-bd080977f54f" :question/math-academy-id "q-99256" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}$ given that $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16.$ \n\n*Hint: The critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<16$ is $(0,0).$*" :question/worked-solution "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=16\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,-6y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x &=8\\lambda x \\\\ -6y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(3+\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=16$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =16\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 4).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=16$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2} & =16\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 4),\\,(\\pm 2,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 4)$ | $(\\pm 2,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $-48$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,\\pm 4),$ and its minimum value is $-48.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198405 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bbc1e6a-c728-5c5a-84f8-06a92c30a023" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With No Inner Critical Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c47837c-91e7-575e-bb0e-eeba6e46e885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361251} {:answer/id #uuid "70afc2df-aa10-56b3-bc23-c23074c89c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361336} {:answer/id #uuid "8c0908b9-98ee-5adc-877d-81b2bba50711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361454} {:answer/id #uuid "9d6f809c-461e-5ba5-8943-29549871ecb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361544} {:answer/id #uuid "b782daff-1cbf-5514-ba1c-f04c88f0cadf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d6f809c-461e-5ba5-8943-29549871ecb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361544} :answer-field/id #uuid "69b59c33-2059-5031-b6c9-48e74e90a78e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7eb8a72b-0beb-5d49-b077-ac32cdeca74e" :question/math-academy-id "q-99274" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=8x-3y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\\le 35.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨8,-3⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=35.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=35\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8,-3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,6y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8 &=4\\lambda x \\\\ -3 &=6\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=-4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-4y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=35,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =35 \\\\ 32{y}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =35 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-4y=∓4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-4,1),\\,(4,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-4,1)$ | $(4,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-35$ | $35$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(4,-1),$ and its maximum value is $35.$"}]}
